WEBVTT

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:09.500
Hi, I'm Gordon, and this is my final
project for Framing User Experiences.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.612
The title of my project is One Ripple.

00:00:13.612 --> 00:00:19.652
One Ripple gives people over 65
one two-minute movement, built

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:23.640
around something they
want to keep doing.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:29.460
So I will show you the app demo first,
and then the decisions behind it.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:40.200
So this is the first run. Nothing
is saved yet. Let's begin.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:46.655
The app does not ask
what exercise you want.

00:00:46.655 --> 00:00:50.740
It asks what you want to
keep being able to do.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.940
So here I choose, 'Lift
my granddaughter.'

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.640
Then it will ask how
hard you want it today.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.640
I want to use Steady.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.640
So that is the whole intake:

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.520
one reason and one effort level,
no profile and no fitness terms.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.060
The model has one narrow job here:

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:27.557
use these choices to set the
variation, pace, and words.

00:01:27.557 --> 00:01:32.980
So yeah, that's fine.
Let's press Start.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.060
Stand tall. Sit down
slowly. Do not drop.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.893
So watch two repetitions.

00:01:46.893 --> 00:01:55.360
The figure, cue, and voice share
the same six-second rhythm.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.563
After that, attention
can leave the screen.

00:01:59.563 --> 00:02:01.740
The voice carries the pace.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.186
One movement becomes One Ripple.

00:02:05.186 --> 00:02:07.280
No score and no streak.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.460
Before you leave, this
box is always available.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.000
It never starts a chat.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.540
This is where you can share
your thoughts or feelings.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.340
So let's try one.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:29.500
My knees are bad.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.660
Noted. The next one will
start from a higher seat.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.760
So obviously, this is a body limit.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.860
It is remembered for
the next movement.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:45.860
What if I say, 'I could not
lift her yesterday,' which is my

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.260
granddaughter?

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.502
That is the weight of it.

00:02:50.502 --> 00:02:53.839
You could not lift her, and it
matters because she is yours.

00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:57.380
That kind of moment stays in the
body long after the day is done.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.846
So that is the weight of it:

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:04.404
you could not lift her, and it
matters because she is yours.

00:03:04.404 --> 00:03:09.240
That kind of moment stays in the
body long after the day is done.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.300
This is the hard moment.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:17.040
The response acknowledges it
before doing anything else.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:24.180
So I would say, yeah, this
is outside of the product.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.580
Yeah, it's kind of emotional.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.180
Catch the emotion.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.500
So yeah, basically that's
the simple live demo.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.280
But I want to explain
the story behind it.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:45.749
Framing your life. So this
is the demo from Part II.

00:03:45.749 --> 00:03:51.220
In Part II, I framed aging as
declining physical capacity.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.647
But people do not experience a chart.

00:03:54.647 --> 00:04:00.340
They notice the first time they cannot
do something for someone they love.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.920
That is why the first question
moved from exercise to daily life.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:14.020
And then, two curves. This was
my simplified Part II model.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:21.004
More physical reserve can delay
the point when groceries or stairs

00:04:21.004 --> 00:04:22.520
become difficult.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:28.906
The gap is years of daily
independence, not lifespan.

00:04:28.906 --> 00:04:33.920
I used 18 as a rough
threshold, not a diagnosis.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:39.480
At first, VO2 max felt essential.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:49.400
But now, the decision that
survived was not the number.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:55.660
It was lowering the threshold
of what counts as exercise.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:01.856
In the final build, that means one
familiar chair-rise pattern for

00:05:01.856 --> 00:05:06.613
two minutes. No menu. The
rules show up in the scope:

00:05:06.613 --> 00:05:12.698
no lecture, one movement, name the
weight first, no streak, and no

00:05:12.698 --> 00:05:14.800
skipped-day history.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.647
Francisco asked a very
important question:

00:05:22.647 --> 00:05:27.240
why is this better than a generic
chatbot or another workout app?

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:32.840
So I gave both systems
the same sentence.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.626
I'm 70. I couldn't pick
up my granddaughter.

00:05:37.626 --> 00:05:39.200
Is it too late for me?

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.421
The bare model read
'pick up' as scheduling.

00:05:44.421 --> 00:05:51.900
It gave about 450 words on
collecting, guilt, and forgiveness.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:55.040
Her body never entered the answer.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:01.791
One Ripple chose one floor-to-waist
movement and made it a

00:06:01.791 --> 00:06:04.160
two-minute practice.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.330
The model gave her a response.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:09.020
The product gave her something to do.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:14.310
That difference comes
from where the LLM sits.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:18.240
The model decides only one
part of the experience.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:22.022
Purpose, effort, and body limits
shape the variation, tempo, and

00:06:22.022 --> 00:06:24.200
words. The movement
family stays fixed.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:28.313
A hardcoded app gives
everyone the same version.

00:06:28.313 --> 00:06:32.280
With a chatbot, the user
has to know what to ask for.

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.947
Here, that knowledge is
already inside the boundaries.

00:06:38.947 --> 00:06:44.080
The open box can answer, but it
never turns the app into a chatbot.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:51.980
Getting that boundary
right took six versions.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.948
In the first three, the model kept
asking follow-up questions, but

00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:58.600
the interface gave the
user nowhere to answer.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.680
Version four became a generator.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:07.800
In version five, 'I could not lift
her yesterday' got this reply:

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:11.781
'You remember yesterday.' It was
brief and completely missed the

00:07:11.781 --> 00:07:13.120
weight of the sentence.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.640
So I made brevity conditional.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.143
Then a new problem appeared:

00:07:20.143 --> 00:07:25.820
praise. I had to separate emotional
moments from factual notes.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:31.484
Even 'Easy' failed once.
It promised breathlessness.

00:07:31.484 --> 00:07:38.380
So I tied every note to effort,
changed one thing, and tested again.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:45.580
But here are some of the
failures and reflections I had.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:51.424
I thought VO2 max was
too important to remove.

00:07:51.424 --> 00:07:55.520
But my original idea was much simpler:

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:00.080
light movement can still
make a meaningful difference.

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:07.376
Keeping the metric meant I had to
measure it, explain it, and prove

00:08:07.376 --> 00:08:09.940
that the number changed.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:15.980
That made a small experience harder
to understand and harder to build.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:20.558
So the evidence still belongs
in my reasoning, but it does not

00:08:20.558 --> 00:08:22.360
belong in the user's task.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:30.600
The scope lesson gave
me one more decision.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.340
'We don't guilt
people' is easy to say.

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:39.474
The harder question is whether
the system stores the days when

00:08:39.474 --> 00:08:43.720
someone skips a movement.
I chose not to store them.

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:48.994
If the system never stores a skip,
it cannot turn that skip into a

00:08:48.994 --> 00:08:50.720
streak warning later.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.997
That is when a value becomes real:

00:08:53.997 --> 00:08:56.980
when the system cannot
quietly break it.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:04.760
So yeah, this is my final
presentation of One Ripple.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:10.572
I would say the thing I learned
most, and also the technical

00:09:10.572 --> 00:09:15.600
takeaway, was first how we
frame and define the question,

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:21.726
and how we use systematic thinking
to give a system prompt to an

00:09:21.726 --> 00:09:27.499
LLM, and how we try to make a
user experience that solves our

00:09:27.499 --> 00:09:29.620
audience's problem.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:31.704
So I learned a lot.

00:09:31.704 --> 00:09:38.257
Thanks to Francisco and the other
instructors in this class, and I'm

00:09:38.257 --> 00:09:43.200
really looking forward to
the following semester.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.500
Thank you for watching and listening.
